In this example, we demonstrate how to handle missing values and outliers in the dataset before performing feature selection and regularization to improve the accuracy of the model. The `OLSMultipleLinearRegression` class provides methods for estimating regression parameters and computing the coefficients.

output:
To handle missing values, we can either remove the entire row or impute the missing values with a suitable value. To remove the entire row, we can use the `Arrays.stream()` method to filter out rows that contain missing values. To impute missing values, we can use techniques such as mean imputation or regression imputation.
To handle outliers, we can use techniques such as Winsorization, where we replace extreme values with the nearest non-extreme value. Another technique is to use robust regression, which is less sensitive to outliers.
To perform feature selection, we can use techniques such as forward selection or backward elimination. In forward selection, we start with a single feature and add features one by one until the model's performance stops improving. In backward elimination, we start with all features and remove one feature at a time until the model's performance stops improving.
To perform regularization, we can use techniques such as L1 regularization (Lasso) or L2 regularization (Ridge). L1 regularization adds a penalty term to the cost function that encourages sparsity in the coefficients, while L2 regularization adds a penalty term that encourages small coefficients.
Here is an updated code example that demonstrates how to handle missing values and outliers, perform feature selection and regularization, and estimate the regression parameters using the `OLSMultipleLinearRegression` class from the Apache Commons Math library:
```java
import org.apache.commons.math3.stat.regression.OLSMultipleLinearRegression;
import java.util.Arrays;
import java.util.stream.DoubleStream;
public class MultipleLinearRegressionExample {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        double[][] data = {{1, 2, 3}, {2, 3, 4}, {3, 5, 6}, {4, 7, 8}, {5, 11, 12}};
        // Handle missing values by imputing with mean
        for (int i = 0; i < data[0].length; i++) {
            final int j = i;
            double[] column = Arrays.stream(data).mapToDouble(row -> row[j]).toArray();
            double mean = DoubleStream.of(column).filter(d -> !Double.isNaN(d)).average().getAsDouble();
            for (int k = 0; k < column.length; k++) {
                if (Double.isNaN(column[k])) {
                    column[k] = mean;
                }
            }
            for (int k = 0; k < data.length; k++) {
                data[k][j] = column[k];
            }
        }
        // Handle outliers by Winsorization
        for (int i = 0; i < data[0].length; i++) {
            final int j = i;
            double[] column = Arrays.stream(data).mapToDouble(row -> row[j]).toArray();
            double q1 = getPercentile(column, 25);
            double q3 = getPercentile(column, 75);
            double iqr = q3 - q1;
            double lower = q1 - 1.5 * iqr;
            double upper = q3 + 1.5 * iqr;
            for (int k = 0; k < column.length; k++) {
                if (column[k] < lower) {
                    column[k] = lower;
                } else if (column[k] > upper) {
                    column[k] = upper;
                }
            }
            for (int k = 0; k < data.length; k++) {
                data[k][j] = column[k];
            }
        }
        // Perform feature selection using forward selection
        double[][] selectedData = new double[data.length][1];
        for (int i = 0; i < data.length; i++) {
            selectedData[i][0] = data[i][0];
        }
        double bestR2 = 0;
        for (int i = 1; i < data[0].length; i++) {
            double[][] candidateData = new double[data.length][i + 1];
            for (int j = 0; j <= i; j++) {
                for (int k = 0; k < data.length; k++) {
                    candidateData[k][j] = data[k][j];
                }
            }
            OLSMultipleLinearRegression candidateRegression = new OLSMultipleLinearRegression();
            candidateRegression.newSampleData(candidateData);
            double candidateR2 = candidateRegression.calculateRSquared();
            if (candidateR2 > bestR2) {
                bestR2 = candidateR2;
                selectedData = candidateData;
            }
        }
        // Perform regularization using L1 regularization
        OLSMultipleLinearRegression regression = new OLSMultipleLinearRegression();
        regression.newSampleData(selectedData);
        double lambda = 0.1;
        double[] coefficients = regression.estimateRegressionParameters(lambda);
        for (double coefficient : coefficients) {
            System.out.println("Coefficient: " + coefficient);
        }
    }
    private static double getPercentile(double[] data, double percentile) {
        Arrays.sort(data);
        int index = (int) Math.ceil(percentile * data.length / 100);
        return data[index - 1];
    }
}
```
In this example, we first handle missing values by imputing with the mean and handle outliers by Winsorization. We then perform feature selection using forward selection and select the best set of features based on the model's performance. Finally, we perform regularization using L1 regularization and estimate the regression parameters. The `estimateRegressionParameters()` method now takes a regularization parameter `lambda` as input, which controls the strength of the regularization. The output shows the estimated coefficients for the selected features.

