Sure, here's the Swift code to reverse the order of sentences in a given text:
```swift
func reverseSentences(_ text: String) -> String {
    var sentences = [String]()
    var currentSentence = ""

    // Split the text into sentences
    for char in text {
        if char == "." {
            sentences.append(currentSentence.trimmingCharacters(in: .whitespacesAndNewlines))
            currentSentence = ""
        } else {
            currentSentence.append(char)
        }
    }

    // Add the last sentence to the array
    if currentSentence != "" {
        sentences.append(currentSentence.trimmingCharacters(in: .whitespacesAndNewlines))
    }

    // Reverse the order of sentences
    var reversedText = ""
    for i in stride(from: sentences.count - 1, through: 0, by: -1) {
        reversedText += sentences[i]
        if i != 0 {
            reversedText += ". "
        }
    }

    return reversedText
}
// Example usage
let text = "By investing in renewable energy, we can reduce our carbon footprint. This will contribute to a healthier planet."
let reversedText = reverseSentences(text)
print(reversedText) // This will contribute to a healthier planet. By investing in renewable energy, we can reduce our carbon footprint.
```
This code first splits the input text into an array of sentences. It then reverses the order of the sentences and concatenates them into a new string. Finally, it returns the reversed string. Note that this code assumes that each sentence in the input text ends with a period followed by a space. If this is not always the case, the code will need to be modified accordingly.

