In C++, you can differentiate between relative and absolute paths using the following criteria:
- Absolute paths: These are paths that start from the root directory of the file system. They provide the complete path to a file or directory, starting from the root directory. Absolute paths always begin with a forward slash (/) on Unix-based systems or a drive letter (e.g. C:\) on Windows systems. 
- Relative paths: These are paths that are relative to the current working directory of the program. They provide a path to a file or directory relative to the current working directory. Relative paths do not begin with a forward slash or drive letter.
The main difference between absolute and relative paths is that absolute paths provide the complete path to a file or directory, while relative paths provide a path relative to the current working directory. 
Here's an example of how to use these paths in C++ using [Shell cmd] data format:
```c++
#include <iostream>
#include <filesystem>
namespace fs = std::filesystem;
int main()
{
    // Absolute path
    fs::path absolutePath = "/home/user/documents/file.txt";
    std::cout << "Absolute path: " << absolutePath << std::endl;
    // Relative path
    fs::path relativePath = "documents/file.txt";
    std::cout << "Relative path: " << relativePath << std::endl;
    // Get current working directory
    fs::path currentPath = fs::current_path();
    std::cout << "Current working directory: " << currentPath << std::endl;
    // Combine current working directory with relative path
    fs::path combinedPath = currentPath / relativePath;
    std::cout << "Combined path: " << combinedPath << std::endl;
    return 0;
}
```
In a networked environment with multiple machines, absolute paths are typically used to specify the location of files or directories that are shared across multiple machines. This is because absolute paths provide a consistent path that can be used by all machines, regardless of their current working directory. Relative paths, on the other hand, are typically used for files or directories that are located on the local machine, as they provide a path relative to the current working directory of the program.

