    public ProcurementCycle() {
        stages = new ArrayList<>();
        actors = new ArrayList<>();
        actorStages = new HashMap<>();
    }
    public void addStage(ProcurementStage stage) {
        stages.add(stage);
    }
    public void addActor(Actor actor) {
        actors.add(actor);
    }
    public void assignActorToStage(Actor actor, ProcurementStage stage) {
        if (!actorStages.containsKey(actor)) {
            actorStages.put(actor, new ArrayList<>());
        }
        actorStages.get(actor).add(stage);
    }
    public void runCycle() {
        for (Actor actor : actors) {
            List<ProcurementStage> assignedStages = actorStages.get(actor);
            for (ProcurementStage stage : assignedStages) {
                stage.execute(actor);
            }
        }
    }
}
public interface ProcurementStage {
    void execute(Actor actor);
}
public interface Actor {
    String getName();
}
public class NeedIdentificationStage implements ProcurementStage {
    public void execute(Actor actor) {
        System.out.println(actor.getName() + " is identifying procurement needs.");
    }
}
public class SupplierIdentificationStage implements ProcurementStage {
    public void execute(Actor actor) {
        System.out.println(actor.getName() + " is identifying and selecting suppliers.");
    }
}
public class ContractNegotiationStage implements ProcurementStage {
    public void execute(Actor actor) {
        System.out.println(actor.getName() + " is negotiating and creating contracts.");
    }
}
public class PurchaseOrderCreationStage implements ProcurementStage {
    public void execute(Actor actor) {
        System.out.println(actor.getName() + " is creating and approving purchase orders.");
    }
}
public class GoodsReceiptStage implements ProcurementStage {
    public void execute(Actor actor) {
        System.out.println(actor.getName() + " is receiving and inspecting goods.");
    }
}
public class InvoiceProcessingStage implements ProcurementStage {
    public void execute(Actor actor) {
        System.out.println(actor.getName() + " is processing invoices and payments.");
    }
}
public class ContractManagementStage implements ProcurementStage {
    public void execute(Actor actor) {
        System.out.println(actor.getName() + " is managing contracts and tracking performance.");
    }
}
public class LegalTeam implements Actor {
    public String getName() {
        return "Legal Team";
    }
}
public class FinanceTeam implements Actor {
    public String getName() {
        return "Finance Team";
    }
}
public class ProcurementTeam implements Actor {
    public String getName() {
        return "Procurement Team";
    }
}
public class Supplier implements Actor {
    private String name;
    public Supplier(String name) {
        this.name = name;
    }
    public String getName() {
        return name;
    }
}
public class Example {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        ProcurementCycle cycle = new ProcurementCycle();
        // Add stages to the cycle
        cycle.addStage(new NeedIdentificationStage());
        cycle.addStage(new SupplierIdentificationStage());
        cycle.addStage(new ContractNegotiationStage());
        cycle.addStage(new PurchaseOrderCreationStage());
        cycle.addStage(new GoodsReceiptStage());
        cycle.addStage(new InvoiceProcessingStage());
        cycle.addStage(new ContractManagementStage());
        // Add actors to the cycle
        cycle.addActor(new LegalTeam());
        cycle.addActor(new FinanceTeam());
        cycle.addActor(new ProcurementTeam());
        cycle.addActor(new Supplier("Supplier A"));
        cycle.addActor(new Supplier("Supplier B"));
        // Assign actors to stages
        cycle.assignActorToStage(new LegalTeam(), new ContractNegotiationStage());
        cycle.assignActorToStage(new FinanceTeam(), new InvoiceProcessingStage());
        cycle.assignActorToStage(new ProcurementTeam(), new NeedIdentificationStage());
        cycle.assignActorToStage(new ProcurementTeam(), new SupplierIdentificationStage());
        cycle.assignActorToStage(new ProcurementTeam(), new ContractNegotiationStage());
        cycle.assignActorToStage(new ProcurementTeam(), new PurchaseOrderCreationStage());
        cycle.assignActorToStage(new ProcurementTeam(), new GoodsReceiptStage());
        cycle.assignActorToStage(new ProcurementTeam(), new InvoiceProcessingStage());
        cycle.assignActorToStage(new ProcurementTeam(), new ContractManagementStage());
        cycle.assignActorToStage(new Supplier("Supplier A"), new GoodsReceiptStage());
        cycle.assignActorToStage(new Supplier("Supplier A"), new InvoiceProcessingStage());
        cycle.assignActorToStage(new Supplier("Supplier B"), new GoodsReceiptStage());
        cycle.assignActorToStage(new Supplier("Supplier B"), new InvoiceProcessingStage());
        // Run the procurement cycle
        cycle.runCycle();
    }
}
```
In this example code, the `ProcurementCycle` class represents the overall procurement cycle and contains lists of stages and actors involved in the cycle. The `ProcurementStage` interface represents a single stage in the procurement cycle, and the `Actor` interface represents an actor involved in the procurement cycle.
The various stages in the procurement cycle are represented by concrete implementations of the `ProcurementStage` interface, such as `NeedIdentificationStage`, `SupplierIdentificationStage`, etc. Each stage has an `execute` method that takes an `Actor` parameter and performs the actions associated with that stage.
The various actors involved in the procurement cycle are represented by concrete implementations of the `Actor` interface, such as `LegalTeam`, `FinanceTeam`, `ProcurementTeam`, and `Supplier`. Each actor has a `getName` method that returns the name of the actor.
The `Example` class demonstrates how the various stages and actors can be added to the procurement cycle and assigned to each other. Finally, the `runCycle` method of the `ProcurementCycle` class is called to execute the entire procurement cycle.

