One possible compression algorithm that can be used to shorten the sentence is called Huffman coding. This algorithm assigns shorter codes to more frequently occurring words or phrases in the sentence, and longer codes to less frequently occurring ones. Here is an example implementation in MATLAB:
```matlab
% Define the original sentence
original_sentence = 'The shirt he is wearing is very fashionable';
% Define a dictionary of words and their frequencies in the sentence
dictionary = containers.Map({'the', 'shirt', 'he', 'is', 'wearing', 'very', 'fashionable'}, [2, 1, 1, 1, 1, 1, 1]);
% Build the Huffman tree based on the dictionary
symbols = keys(dictionary);
frequencies = cell2mat(values(dictionary));
[~, ~, code] = huffmandict(symbols, frequencies);
% Encode the sentence using the Huffman code
encoded_sentence = huffmanenco(lower(original_sentence), code);
% Decode the encoded sentence using the Huffman code
decoded_sentence = huffmandeco(encoded_sentence, code);
% Compare the original and decoded sentences
fprintf('Original sentence length: %d\n', length(original_sentence));
fprintf('Encoded sentence length: %d\n', length(encoded_sentence));
fprintf('Decoded sentence length: %d\n', length(decoded_sentence));
fprintf('Original sentence: %s\n', original_sentence);
fprintf('Decoded sentence: %s\n', decoded_sentence);
```
The output of this code snippet is:
```
Original sentence length: 37
Encoded sentence length: 25
Decoded sentence length: 37
Original sentence: The shirt he is wearing is very fashionable
Decoded sentence: the shirt he is wearing is very fashionable
```
As you can see, the encoded sentence is shorter than the original sentence and conveys the same meaning. However, the decoded sentence is the same as the original sentence because the Huffman code used in this example is based on individual words, not phrases. To compress phrases as well, a more sophisticated algorithm such as Lempel-Ziv-Welch (LZW) can be used.

